{{also|trans-gender}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2024|March|31}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*terh₂-|*ǵenh₁-}}
The {{glossary|adjective}} is derived from {{prefix|en|trans|pos1={{glossary|prefix}} meaning ‘extending across, through, or over’|gender}}, modelled after {{m|en|transsexual|pos=adjective}}<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=adj.'' and ''n|id=1951538889|date=December 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220626083636/https://www.lexico.com/definition/transgender}}</ref> and probably modified from {{m|en|transgenderism}} which was {{coinage|en|John F. Oliven|w=-|nat=the American|occ=psychiatrist|nobycat=1|nocap=1}} (1915–1975) in 1965;<ref>{{cite-book|en|author=John F. Oliven|chapter=Sexual Deviations|title=Sexual Hygiene and Pathology: A Manual for the Physician and the Professions|edition=2nd|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=[[w:J. B. Lippincott & Co.|J[oshua] B[allinger] Lippincott & Co.]]|year=1965|page=514|pageurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220816104558/https://i2.wp.com/research.cristanwilliams.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/photo1.jpg|oclc=804038|passage=''Transexualism''. Where the compulsive urge reaches beyond female vestments, and becomes an urge for gender (“sex”) change, transvestism becomes “transexualism.” The term is misleading; actually, “transgenderism” is meant, because sexuality is not a major factor in primary transvestism. Psychologically, the transexual often differs from the simple cross-dresser; he is conscious at all times of a strong desire to be a woman, and the urge can be truly consuming.}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|author=Thomas E. Bevan|chapter=Definitions and Population Frequencies|title=The Psychobiology of Transsexualism and Transgenderism: A New View Based on Scientific Evidence|location=Santa Barbara, Calif.|publisher=Praeger, [[w:ABC-Clio|ABC-CLIO]]|year=2014|page=42|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/psychobiologyoft0000beva/page/42/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-4408-3126-3|passage=The term ''transsexual'' was introduced by Cauldwell (1949) and popularized by Harry Benjamin (1966) {{...}} The term ''transgender'' was coined by John Oliven (1965) and was popularized by various transgender people who pioneered the concept and practice of transgenderism. It is sometimes said that Virginia Prince (1976) popularized the term, but history shows that many transgender people advocated the use of the term much more than Prince. {{...}} Transsexuals constitute a subset of transgender people.}}</ref><ref name="Erie Gay News">{{cite-journal|en|author=Ray Simon|title=Stirring up the Origin of the ‘Alphabet Soup’|journal={{w|Erie Gay News}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20211111041638/https://www.eriegaynews.com/news/article.php?recordid=201711lgbtalphabetsouporigin|location=Erie, Pa.|publisher=EGC Coalition|month=November|year=2017|issn=2159-1792|oclc=694108900|passage=According to scholars, the word [''transgender''] first appeared in print in John F. Oliven’s 1965 book, “Sexual Hygiene and Pathology.” Oliven writes: / Where the compulsive urge reaches beyond female vestments, and becomes an urge for gender (‘sex’) change, transvestism becomes ‘transsexualism.’ The term is misleading; actually, ‘transgenderism’ is what is meant, because sexuality is not a major factor in primary transvestism. / Although Oliven’s understanding of “transgender” is not the same as our understanding of it today, his use of it is still significant. As K. J. Rawson and Cristan Williams note in their book, “Transgender*: The Rhetorical Landscape of a Term,” Oliven didn’t use the word in the book’s 1955 edition; it was added later, when the second edition was revised and published. / Gradually, some members of this marginalized community began to apply the word “transgender” to themselves. For example, Virginia Charles Prince, publisher of the long-running periodical “Transvestia,” occasionally used a variation of the word, “transgenderal.”}}</ref> the terms {{m|en|transgenderal}}, {{m|en|transgendered}}, {{m|en|transgenderist}}, and similar terms arose in the decades after this.<ref name="Erie Gay News"/> By the 1990s, the word ''transgender'' had acquired its current senses, and had also largely displaced the earlier term ''transsexual'': see the [[#Usage notes|usage notes]].<ref name="TR2006">{{cite-book|author={{w|Paisley Currah}}|chapter=Gender Pluralisms under the Transgender Umbrella|editors=Paisley Currah, Richard M. Juang, Shannon Price Minter|title=Transgender Rights|year=2006|section=part I (Law)|page=4|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/transgenderright0000unse/page/4/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-8166-4311-0|passage=From signifying a subject position between cross-dresser and transsexual, the meaning of ''transgender'' expanded radically in the early 1990s to include them, along with other cross-gender practices and identities.}}</ref>

The {{glossary|noun}} and {{glossary|verb}} are derived from the adjective.<ref name="OED"/> Regarding noun {{senseno|en|transgenderism}} (“synonym of ''transgenderism''”), compare {{m|en|transsex#Noun|pos=noun}}.

