#: {{synonyms|en|transgenderal<q:uncommon>}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jerry Parker|title=A woman with no regrets|editor=Stanley Green|journal=[[w:Newsday|LI: Newsday’s Magazine for Long Island]]|edition=Suffolk|location=Melville, N.Y.|publisher=Newsday Inc.|date=23 September 1979|page=18|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/article/newsday-suffolk-edition-transgender/143432227/|columns=3–4|issn=0278-5587|oclc=54839840|passage="If you understand '''trans-genders'''," she &#91;{{w|Christine Jorgensen}}&#93; says, choosing the word she prefers to transsexuals, "then you understand that gender is different than sexual preference. It doesn't have to do with bed partners, it has to do with identity." Jorgensen says she knows of some male-to-female '''trans-genders''' who have settled into lesbian relationships. She herself is heterosexual.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Sue-Ellen Jacobs; Christine Roberts|chapter=Sex, Sexuality, Gender, and Gender Variance|editor=w:Sandra Morgen|title=Gender and Anthropology: Critical Reviews for Research and Teaching|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=w:American Anthropological Association|year=1989|page=455|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/genderanthropolo0000unse/page/455/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-913167-33-5|passage='''''transgender'''''—an individual who lives as a person of a gender different from the one society defines for that person's sex (e.g., male "transvestites" who wear women's clothing, hair styles and other body accoutrements, use "feminine" speech and body language, and identify with the gender category ''woman''). Also sometimes referred to as "cross-gendered people" (Blackwood 1984).|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Walter Bockting; Eric Avery|title=Transgender Health and HIV Prevention: : Needs Assessment Studies from Transgender Communities across the United States|series=[[w:International Journal of Transgender Health|International Journal of Transgenderism]]|seriesvolume=volume 8, issues 2–3|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Haworth Medical Press|year=2005|page=116|isbn=978-0-7890-3015-3|passage=In a patriarchal society in which ''machismo'' rules, MTF [male-to-female] '''transgenders''' represent a challenge to traditional masculinity due to their renouncing of the male position of social power.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Caroline Symons; Dennis Hemphill|chapter=Transgendering Sex and Sport in the Gay Games|editor=Jayne Caudwell|title=Sport, Sexualities and Queer/Theory|series=Routledge Critical Studies in Sport|location=Abingdon, Oxfordshire&#59; New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Routledge|year=2006|page=122|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sportsexualities0000unse/page/122/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-415-36761-5|passage=There were divisions for [netball] teams of men, women, mixed and '''transgenders'''. Individual '''transgenders''' could compete in any division; however, transgender teams could not play against biological women's teams.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Sheila Jeffreys|title=Gender Hurts: A Feminist Analysis of the Politics of Transgenderism|location=Abingdon, Oxfordshire&#59; New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Routledge|year=2014|page=70|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xhRxAwAAQBAJ&pg=PT76|isbn=978-0-415-53939-5|passage=This public presentation of the mutilation of the penis is not obviously very different from the forms of disassembly of the penis engaged in by male body modifiers—particularly nullos and '''transgenders'''—on the Body Modification Ezine website.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Helen Davies|title=Transgender woman forced to move house after death threats and knife in her front door|journal=w:Liverpool Echo|location=Liverpool, Merseyside|publisher=[[w:Reach plc|Trinity Mirror Merseyside]]|year=2015|issn=1751-6277|oclc=500165464|passage=Nat spent years being victimised as a male to female '''transgender''' but was too scared to report it.}}
# {{senseid|en|transgenderism}}{{lb|en|uncountable|rare}} {{synonym of|en|transgenderism|t=the [[state#Noun|state]] of being transgender}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Pierre Bowman|title=Back in showbiz&nbsp;Christine Jorgensen|editor=George Chaplin|journal=w:The Honolulu Advertiser|series=People Report Section|location=Honolulu, Hi.|publisher=w:Thurston Twigg-Smith|date=3 May 1979|page=B-1|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-honolulu-advertiser-back-in-showbiz/36529325/|column=6|issn=1072-7191|oclc=663366710|passage=In spite of not "rootin' and tootin'" as a trans-gender case, in recent years [[w:Christine Jorgensen|[Christine] Jorgensen]] has been a staple on the college lecture circuit, speaking&nbsp;– of course&nbsp;– about '''trans-gender''' and herself.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Leslie Feinberg|title=The love that dared to speak its name|journal=[[w:Workers World (newspaper)|Workers World]]|url=https://archive.org/details/transgender-warriors-making-history-from-joan-of-arc-to-dennis-rodman-feinberg-leslie-1949/Lavender_and_Red/page/n8/mode/1up|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=WW Publishers|date=10 June 2004|issn=1070-4205|oclc=28359687|passage=The Scientific Humanitarian Committee published a yearbook that reported on movement activities. It also documented literary, cross-cultural, cross-historical and scientific studies on same-sex love and '''transgender'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Alison Stone|chapter=Sex|title=An Introduction to Feminist Philosophy|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire&#59; Malden, Mass.|publisher=w:Polity Press|year=2007|page=41|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=UKwG7cpVW1IC&pg=PA41|isbn=978-0-7456-3882-9|passage=Before we can answer this question, we need to consider two other phenomena&nbsp;– transsex and '''transgender'''&nbsp;– which also expose the muddle within conventional categories of sex.}}

====Usage notes====
* In Western countries, many transgender people consider the use of ''transgender'' (and similar adjectives) as a noun to be offensive, and several guides advise against such usage.<ref>{{cite-web|title=transgender|work=[[w:Reuters|The Reuters Style Guide]]|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180801105220/http://handbook.reuters.com/?title=T#transgender|year=2018|passage=Do not use transgender as a noun; no one should be referred to as “a transgender.’}}</ref><ref name="Guardian">{{cite-web|title=transgender, trans|work=[[w:The Guardian|Guardian and Observer Style Guide]]|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313154607/https://www.theguardian.com/guardian-observer-style-guide-t|date=20 May 2021|passage=Where relevant, use transgender {{...}} only as an adjective: transgender person, trans person; never ‘transgendered person’ or ‘a transgender’.}}</ref><ref>{{cite-web|title=[[w:BBC News|BBC News Style Guide]]: Gender/sex|work={{w|BBC Academy}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20171222083800/http://www.bbc.co.uk/academy/journalism/news-style-guide/article/art20130702112133564|year=2017|passage=Do not say ‘transsexuals’, in the same way we would not talk about ‘gays’ or ‘blacks’.}}</ref><ref>{{cite-web|title=Glossary of Terms: Transgender|work=[[w:GLAAD|GLAAD Media Reference Guide 11th Edition]]|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240111183657/https://glaad.org/reference/trans-terms/|year=2024|passage=TERM TO AVOID: “transgenders,” “a transgender”  / ''Transgender'' should be used as an adjective, not as a noun. Do not say, “Tony is a transgender,” or “The parade included many transgenders.” {{...}} BEST PRACTICE: transgender people, a transgender person / For example, “Tony is a transgender man,” or “The parade included many transgender people.”}}</ref> “A transgender man” (for a man who was assigned the female sex at birth) or “a transgender woman” (for the reverse) is frequently more appropriate.

